"""Prose sections (stage 4 draft) for Paper 3, 'How the 2015–16 El Niño developed'.

build(c, d) returns section dicts; c(*keys) renders citation links; d holds sentences built from the diagnostics in
make_2015.py (no hand-typed diagnostic values). Other numbers come from the timeline, evidence table or monthly table.
"""


def build(c, d):
    S = []
    S.append({"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "html": f"""<p>The 2015–16 El Niño peaked at +2.25 on NOAA's Relative Oceanic Niño Index (RONI) in NDJ 2015–16 and at +2.59 on the traditional ONI: on the relative index it is the weakest of the three super events in this series, on the traditional index the strongest, and within observational uncertainty the three cannot be separated {c('cpc', 'huang2016rank')}. Its development was a two-year story. A recharged Pacific and strong westerly bursts in early 2014 set up a widely forecast major event that stalled: westerly bursts stopped from April to June, a basin-wide easterly burst in June knocked the Niño indices back to normal, and a cold south-eastern Pacific diverted the winds {c('menkes2014', 'hufedorov2016', 'zhu2016')}. The same easterly burst stopped the warm water from draining away, leaving the basin charged for 2015 {c('levine2016')}. From March 2015 a strong burst, then an unusually long run of bursts through the year — the most in any year of the 1979–2016 reanalysis — drove four downwelling Kelvin waves and coupled growth to a peak in November–January {c('xuekumar2017', 'r1diag')}. The event was centred further west than 1982–83 and 1997–98, with record Niño 4 and weaker warming at the South American coast, consistent with an added role for central-Pacific, subtropically forced dynamics; a North Pacific coupled mode linked to the 2013–15 'Blob' is a proposed Northern Hemisphere trigger {c('lheureux2017', 'paek2017', 'tseng2017')}. TAO mooring reporting declined sharply in 2012–14, so the failed 2014 onset was observed with far fewer moorings than 1997 {c('legler2014', 'taodata')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "findings", "title": "Main findings", "html": f"""<ol>
<li><b>Precondition (grade A−; high confidence).</b> Warm water volume stayed positive from mid-2013; the June 2014 easterly burst prevented and then reversed the discharge that a 2014 El Niño would have caused, giving 2015 a head start {c('cpc', 'levine2016')}.</li>
<li><b>A failed trigger in 2014 (grade A; high confidence).</b> Two strong bursts in January–March 2014, then none from April to June; a wind-forced ocean model reproduces both the 2014 stall and the 2015 growth from bursts and easterly surges alone {c('menkes2014', 'chiodiharrison2017', 'r1diag')}.</li>
<li><b>Suppressors in 2014 (grade A for the easterly burst, B/C for the meridional gradient).</b> A basin-wide easterly burst in June and a south-eastern Pacific cold anomaly that drove meridional rather than zonal winds {c('hufedorov2016', 'zhu2016')}.</li>
<li><b>Trigger and growth in 2015 (grade A−; high confidence).</b> A strong burst in March 2015 and a record number of bursts through the year drove four downwelling Kelvin waves from April to November {c('r1diag', 'xuekumar2017', 'taodata')}.</li>
<li><b>Pattern (grade B; medium–high confidence).</b> Westward-centred warming, record Niño 4 and a weak coastal thermocline anomaly: eastern-Pacific dynamics plus stronger central-Pacific dynamics {c('xuekumar2017', 'paek2017', 'lheureux2017')}.</li>
<li><b>North Pacific link (grade B/C; medium–low confidence).</b> A Victoria-mode/meridional-mode pathway from the North Pacific is proposed for this event; it rests on one statistical study and general hindcasts {c('tseng2017', 'liang2025')}.</li>
<li><b>Background warming (grade C).</b> The index adjustment lowers the peak by 0.34 °C, the most of the three events; one review attributes the extremity partly to background warming, but no event attribution is made here {c('lheureux2024', 'santoso2017')}.</li>
</ol>"""})

    S.append({"id": "intro", "title": "1. Introduction", "html": f"""<p>Early in 2014 the tropical Pacific looked much as it had in early 1997: a recharged equatorial heat content, an eastward-displaced warm pool and strong westerly bursts. Many models forecast a strong El Niño, some an extreme one {c('menkes2014', 'hufedorov2016')}. It did not happen in 2014; it happened, at extreme strength, in 2015. That makes 2015–16 the only one of the three historical super events with a documented false start, and a natural test of what decides whether a charged Pacific turns into a major El Niño. The forecasts for 2015 itself were good, in contrast to the 'overexuberant' predictions of the year before {c('lheureux2017')}.</p>
<p>This paper reconstructs the sequence from mid-2013 to the La Niña of late 2016 with the same framework, evidence grades and primary index (RONI) as Papers 1 and 2, and with identical reanalysis diagnostics, so that the three events can be compared.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "methods", "title": "2. Definitions, data and methods", "html": f"""<p><b>Index.</b> RONI is the primary index {c('noaa2026', 'lheureux2024')}; ONI is shown alongside. The choice matters more for this event than for the others: removing the tropical-mean warming lowers the peak from +2.59 to +2.25.</p>
<p><b>Observations.</b> Argo floats and satellite altimetry, winds and salinity were all available. The TAO/TRITON array was not: its reporting declined dramatically from mid-2012 to early 2014 {c('legler2014')}. {d.get('tao_sentence', '')}</p>
<p><b>Reanalysis diagnostics.</b> The burst and MJO diagnostics of Papers 1 and 2 were re-run for July 2013 – December 2016 with the same definitions: 1000 hPa anomaly of at least 5 m s⁻¹ for at least 3 days over at least 10° of longitude, 5°S–5°N, 120°E–150°W, against a 1979–2000 climatology, with the MJO index patterns fitted to 1979–2001 {c('r1diag')}. Because easterly bursts are broad rather than intense, they are described with the 150°E–100°W mean anomaly instead of the burst rule. Kelvin waves are traced in TAO 20 °C isotherm depth where the moorings reported.</p>
<p><b>Evidence grades</b> are those of Papers 1 and 2 (A: event observation plus a model or budget study isolating the effect; B: event observation with a plausible mechanism; C: correlation, composite or theory; D: speculative or untested). Several key studies were read only as abstracts; the reference list records what was read.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "preconditions", "title": "3. Pre-event conditions and the false start of 2014", "html": f"""<p><b>A charged Pacific.</b> After weak cool conditions in 2013 (RONI −0.48 in JJA 2013), warm water volume was positive throughout July 2013 – February 2014 (+0.78 to +1.25 × 10¹⁴ m³) {c('cpc', 'meinen2000')}. In winter 2013–14 an exceptional warm anomaly — the 'Blob' — formed in the north-eastern Pacific, the largest there since at least the 1980s, through reduced ocean heat loss and weak cold advection under persistently high pressure {c('bond2015')}.</p>
<p><b>Early 2014: the onset that stalled.</b> {d.get('bursts2014', '')} Heat content east of the Date Line rose from −0.33 °C in January to +1.60 °C in March and warm water volume peaked at +2.05 × 10¹⁴ m³ {c('cpc')}. Until April the warm pool was displaced east as at the onset of strong events, but by July it had retreated: in ocean simulations the difference from 1997 is the absence of westerly events from April to June {c('menkes2014')}. A wind-forced model built from the available buoy winds explains the early development and mid-year reversal by a springtime surplus of westerly events followed by a summer balance between westerly events and easterly surges {c('chiodiharrison2017')}.</p>
<p><b>Suppressors.</b> In June an unusually strong, basin-wide easterly burst preceded a rapid fall of all major Niño indices to near normal; afterwards two separate warm centres persisted in the central and eastern Pacific and the Bjerknes feedback did not engage {c('hufedorov2016')}. {d.get('ewb_sentence', '')} A south-eastern Pacific cold anomaly with warmth to the north drove strong meridional winds near the equator rather than the zonal winds that growth needs; in forecast experiments it accounts for about 40% of the 2014 amplitude error {c('zhu2016')}.</p>
<p><b>The head start.</b> The same easterly burst prevented, and then reversed, the discharge of equatorial heat content that a 2014 El Niño would have caused; warm water volume rebuilt to +1.49 and +1.57 × 10¹⁴ m³ in August–September 2014 {c('levine2016', 'cpc')}. Weak warm conditions persisted (RONI +0.62 in NDJ 2014–15; ONI ≥ +0.5 from SON 2014), but by conventional definitions the 2014 event failed to qualify as an El Niño {c('levine2016')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "chronology", "title": "4. Development, March – December 2015", "html": f"""<p><b>Trigger.</b> {d.get('march2015', '')} From April to November four downwelling Kelvin waves initiated and enhanced the eastern-central warming, and eastward current anomalies advected the warm pool east in spring and summer {c('xuekumar2017')}. A freshwater anomaly in the equatorial Pacific larger than anything in the 2004–2014 Argo record raised steric height in the west and increased eastward surface acceleration at the salinity front {c('gasparin2016')}.</p>
<p><b>Coupling.</b> RONI rose from +0.89 (AMJ) to +1.27 (JJA), with SOI −0.6 to −1.1 and Niño 1+2 reaching +2.48 °C in July; public El Niño advisories were issued in the first half of 2015 {c('cpc', 'lheureux2017')}. {d.get('bursts2015', '')}</p>
<p><b>Peak.</b> RONI reached +2.03 (SON), +2.18 (OND) and +2.25 (NDJ); central-Pacific OLR fell to −49.9 W m⁻² in December {c('cpc')}. Niño 4 set a record and Niño 3 matched the earlier super events, but Niño 1+2 was much weaker; enhanced convection lay about 20° further west, and the maximum thermocline anomaly near South America was only a third to a half of that in 1982 and 1997 {c('xuekumar2017')}. Unlike previous major El Niños, SST anomalies were remarkably high in the western and central equatorial Pacific and milder near the coast of South America {c('lheureux2017')}. The 1997–98 event followed eastern-Pacific dynamics based on basin-wide thermocline changes; 2015–16 added central-Pacific dynamics dependent on subtropical forcing {c('paek2017', 'kao2009')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "termination", "title": "5. Termination and transition, November 2015 – 2016", "html": f"""<p>An upwelling Kelvin wave emerged in early November 2015 and led to a rapid decline {c('xuekumar2017')}; warm water volume turned negative in December (−0.39 × 10¹⁴ m³) and fell to −3.36 × 10¹⁴ m³ by April 2016 {c('cpc')}. RONI fell from +1.81 (JFM) to +0.08 (AMJ) and the event ended in May 2016 {c('cpc', 'lheureux2017')}. During decay the SST anomalies lingered around the Date Line instead of retreating to the South American coast as in 1997–98 {c('paek2017')}. The southward shift of westerly anomalies in boreal winter, tied to the seasonal strengthening of the South Pacific Convergence Zone, is a key element in the termination of strong El Niño events in general {c('mcgregor2012')}; its role in 2016 was not isolated in the studies read here. A weak La Niña followed (RONI −0.96 in SON 2016), promoted by a strong negative Indian Ocean Dipole that developed mainly from Indian Ocean conditions {c('lim2017iod')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "interactions", "title": "6. Interactions with the broader climate system", "html": f"""<p><b>Intraseasonal variability.</b> {d.get('mjo_paragraph', '')} Every El Niño of 1971–2010 was accompanied by clustered westerly bursts, and whether they produce a central- or eastern-Pacific event depends on their timing within the recharge–discharge cycle {c('lian2014')}; in 2015 they fell on a basin already recharged by the aborted 2014 event.</p>
<p><b>North Pacific.</b> The Blob persisted for several years and the PDO was positive from 2014 (up to +1.86 in December 2014) {c('dilorenzo2016', 'cpc')}. One analysis identifies the Blob as part of an enhanced Victoria-mode coupled pattern forced by a North Pacific Oscillation-like circulation from 2013, a precursor of ENSO that bridged to the 2015–16 El Niño, which it describes as triggered from the Northern Hemisphere, unlike other large events {c('tseng2017')}. For the earlier super events a Southern Hemisphere circulation 'booster' had been identified {c('hong2014')}. Hindcast experiments find that the North Pacific Oscillation-induced meridional mode influences ENSO evolution more lastingly than a March MJO {c('liang2025')}. The evidence for 2015 itself remains statistical (grade B/C).</p>
<p><b>South Pacific.</b> The 2014 south-eastern Pacific cold anomaly acted as a suppressor (above) {c('zhu2016')}.</p>
<p><b>Indian Ocean.</b> The dipole index was weakly positive in August–October 2015 (CPC DMI +0.42 to +0.47 °C) {c('cpc')}; IOD phase is largely set by ENSO through combination-mode wind and heat-flux forcing {c('stuecker2017')}. The strong negative IOD of 2016 mattered for the transition: without it the 2016 La Niña may have been substantially weaker {c('lim2017iod')}.</p>
<p><b>Volcanic forcing.</b> {d.get('volcano_sentence', '')}</p>
<p><b>Background warming.</b> The tropical-mean adjustment lowers the peak by 0.34 °C (ONI +2.59, RONI +2.25), the largest difference of the three events {c('cpc', 'lheureux2024')}. A review attributes the extremity of the event in part to the unusually warm 2014 conditions and to long-term background warming {c('santoso2017')}; the rapid global temperature rise of 2014–16 has been explained by heat release from these El Niño conditions on top of the warming trend {c('hufedorov2017')}. With SST uncertainty the three strongest events are not separable {c('huang2016rank')}. No event-specific attribution of amplitude is made here.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "synthesis", "title": "7. Causal synthesis and competing explanations", "html": f"""<p>The most plausible sequence is: (1) the equatorial Pacific was recharged from 2013; (2) early-2014 bursts started an onset that stalled without spring bursts, under a June easterly burst and a cold south-eastern Pacific; (3) the easterly burst kept the heat in the basin; (4) a strong burst in March 2015 and a record run of bursts through 2015 drove repeated Kelvin waves and coupled growth; (5) central-Pacific dynamics and a North Pacific precursor gave the event its westward-shifted pattern; (6) an upwelling Kelvin wave from November 2015 and discharge ended it by May 2016.</p>
<p>Competing views concern weights rather than ingredients: whether the 2014 stall was mainly the missing spring bursts {c('menkes2014')}, the easterly burst {c('hufedorov2016')}, a balance of both {c('chiodiharrison2017')} or the meridional SST gradient {c('zhu2016')}; and whether the North Pacific precursor was a cause or a parallel response to the same atmospheric forcing {c('tseng2017')}. Many 2015 bursts came after coupling had begun and are probably partly a response to it {c('eisenman2005', 'liang2021gl')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "knowable", "title": "8. What was forecast at the time", "html": f"""<p>The predictions made in 2014 were 'overexuberant': models anticipated a strong event that did not develop {c('lheureux2017')}. A climate model with superimposed wind bursts suggests inherent limits to predictability once an easterly burst of this kind occurs {c('hufedorov2016')}. In 2015 the life cycle and magnitude were well predicted by most models used by national meteorological services {c('lheureux2017')}.</p>"""})

    S.append({"id": "uncertainty", "title": "9. Uncertainties", "html": f"""<ul>
<li>TAO data return in 2014 was poor, so wind-forced reconstructions of the 2014 stall depend on gap-filled winds {c('chiodiharrison2017', 'taodata')}.</li>
<li>The burst count uses a 1979–2000 climatology; any trend in the background winds since then affects counts in 2014–16.</li>
<li>Several key studies (Xue and Kumar; Tseng et al.; Gasparin and Roemmich; Lim and Hendon) were read only as abstracts.</li>
<li>The North Pacific link rests mainly on one statistical analysis.</li>
</ul>"""})

    S.append({"id": "conclusion", "title": "10. Conclusion", "html": f"""<p>The 2015–16 El Niño was built over two years. The ocean was ready in 2014, but the winds were not: without spring bursts, and with a strong June easterly burst and a cold south-eastern Pacific, the 2014 onset stalled. That failure kept the heat in place, and in 2015 a sustained, record run of westerly bursts turned it into an extreme, westward-shifted El Niño with a weaker coastal signature than 1982–83 or 1997–98. Compared with those events the trigger is again the westerly bursts, the precondition again the recharge — and what differs is the path from one to the other.</p>"""})
    return S
